            "content": "## Problem\n\nAfter compaction, the model loses its own verbatim history. `buildContext` excludes every message at or below the compaction's `uptoSeq` and replaces them with a `conversation-checkpoint` summary. That summary is lossy by construction — it is a structured précis, not a transcript — so any detail the summarizer did not judge important is gone from the model's view for the rest of the chat.\n\nThe detail is not gone from the **database**. Those messages are still stored, still owner-scoped, still indexed by the search projection. But `search_conversations` takes only `query` and `limit` (`apps/api/src/tools/search-conversations.ts`), with no way to scope a search to a single chat — so the model cannot ask the one question that would recover it: *\"what did we actually say earlier in this conversation?\"*\n\nThe result is a capability gap that gets worse the longer and more valuable a chat becomes. A long working session compacts, and from that moment the assistant is reasoning about its own conversation through a summary it cannot go behind, while the primary source sits one query away.\n\n## Outcome\n\n`search_conversations` accepts an optional chat filter, and the compaction checkpoint tells the model that the messages it superseded remain retrievable through that filter — so a compacted chat degrades from \"history replaced by a summary\" to \"history summarized, with the transcript still addressable.\"\n\n## Scope\n\n- **An optional chat-scope argument** on `search_conversations`. When present, results are restricted to that chat; when absent, behavior is byte-identical to today.\n- **The filter narrows, it never widens.** Owner scope continues to come from `context.userId`, injected by the run loop and never a model argument. A chat id naming another owner's chat SHALL return nothing, enforced by the existing row-level security rather than by an application check alone, with the in-query owner predicate retained as defense-in-depth. This is the security-critical property of the whole change: the model is being handed an identifier-shaped input for the first time on this tool.\n- **The model must learn its own chat id from a trusted source.** It is not in the digest (deliberately — see #307) and must not be taken from anything the model or a user can author. The natural carrier is the compaction checkpoint itself, which is server-authored: the checkpoint's framing states that earlier messages of *this* chat remain searchable and names the id to use. That keeps the id out of every prompt that does not need it and puts it exactly where it becomes useful.\n- **Decide and document** whether the filter accepts one chat or several. One is sufficient for the motivating case and keeps the schema minimal; several invites the model to fan out across the corpus, which is what the unscoped search is already for.\n- **Ranking within a chat-scoped search.** The shipped ordering is pure relevance with a recency/id tie-break. Within a single chat, chronological order may serve recall better than relevance — decide deliberately rather than inheriting.\n- The existing `searchByOwner` path is reused; no parallel query is introduced.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] With no chat filter supplied, results and ordering are identical to today.\n- [ ] With a filter naming the owner's own chat, only that chat's messages are returned.\n- [ ] A filter naming another owner's chat returns nothing, proven in the row-level-security integration suite with the identity set to the non-owner and again to the empty identity.\n- [ ] A filter naming a nonexistent or malformed id fails as a structured tool observation, not a thrown exception.\n- [ ] After a compaction, the checkpoint states that superseded messages remain retrievable and names the chat to scope to.\n- [ ] The model can recover a specific detail from a pre-compaction message in its own chat, asserted end to end.\n- [ ] The tool remains `read_only` and its gating is unchanged.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNo new query path, no semantic or embedding retrieval, no change to the corpus boundary (system prompts, tool payloads, and reasoning stay excluded), no write capability, and no cross-owner reach. Not a rewind or partial-decompaction mechanism — the checkpoint remains the model's primary view and this is a retrieval escape hatch beneath it.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- #307 drops chat ids from the recency digest precisely because nothing consumed them. This issue is the first consumer, and it deliberately sources the id from the server-authored checkpoint rather than from an injected list.\n- #327 (`recent_chats`) returns chat ids in its results, making it the second consumer; the two should agree on the identifier's shape.\n- Model-generated slugs as a cheaper, more legible identifier than a UUID are being explored separately; if that lands, this filter should accept whatever identifier that work settles on.\n- #326 owns the owner-scoped gate over `search_conversations`; this change inherits it unchanged.\n",
