          "input": {
            "content": "## Problem\n\nProjects exist as storage and as a listing filter, and nowhere else. `chats.project_id` is set, `ChatsRepository.findByOwner` filters on it, the projects page lists them — but **inside a chat, a project is invisible to everyone**. The user sees no indication of which project the conversation belongs to, and the model has no idea projects exist at all.\n\nThat makes the feature inert where it would be worth the most. A project is the single strongest piece of topical context llame holds about a chat, because unlike anything inferred it is the **user's own grouping**, stated deliberately. Filing is also the step users reliably skip: chats get created from the sidebar in the moment, and the decision about where a conversation belongs is obvious only once it has a shape — by which point nothing prompts anyone to make it.\n\nThis is the concrete reason #307's recency digest ships without project names. Annotating digest entries with a project the model can neither see elsewhere, reason about, nor act on would be decoration.\n\n## Outcome\n\nProjects become a first-class part of the chat experience for both participants: the user can see and change a chat's project without leaving the conversation, and the model can see the current chat's project, use it as context, and — when the conversation makes it evident — **propose filing the chat into a project**, with the user deciding.\n\nThis reads as an epic rather than a single slice. The sub-surfaces below are separable and should become their own issues once the shape is agreed.\n\n## Scope\n\nCandidate sub-surfaces, roughly in dependency order:\n\n- **Project visible in the chat UI**, with the ability to file or move a chat from inside the conversation. Everything below assumes a user can act on a suggestion once it is made.\n- **The current chat's project reaches the model as context** — name, and possibly a project-level description or instructions if projects grow those. This is the prerequisite for the model reasoning about projects at all.\n- **The model can propose filing the chat into an existing project**, as a suggestion the user accepts or dismisses. Whether this is a tool call, a typed part, or a UI-side heuristic over the conversation is exactly the design question this epic exists to answer.\n- **The model can propose creating a new project** when a conversation clearly belongs to a grouping that does not exist yet. Strictly harder — it is a creation, not a classification — and probably a later slice.\n- **Project membership enriches recall surfaces**: digest entries, `search_conversations` results, and `recent_chats` results annotated with their project, once the model can do something with the annotation.\n- **Project-scoped recall**: when the current chat is in a project, prefer that project's chats. This is a **ranking change** and deliberately out of #307's recency-only boundary, so it needs its own justification and probably its own evidence.\n\n## Constraints that shape it\n\n- **Anything the model proposes is a proposal.** Filing a chat is a mutation of the user's own organization, and the shipped tool boundary is read-only for good reasons — write-capable tools need checkpoint-or-dedupe semantics because queue retries restart the tool loop from the first step. A suggestion the user confirms sidesteps that; an autonomous move does not.\n- **Projects are owner-only today.** Any surface added here inherits that and must not become the path by which project membership leaks across owners.\n- **Project names are user-authored free text**, so anywhere one reaches a prompt it takes the same tag-sanitizer treatment as personalization and chat titles.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNot a change to the project data model, its row-level security, or the org-unit hierarchy. Not automatic filing without user confirmation. Not project-scoped ranking in recall surfaces — that is called out above as needing separate justification, not as included work.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- #307 (recency digest) omits project names for the reason stated above; it becomes worth revisiting once the model can see and act on projects.\n- #327 (`recent_chats`) would annotate results the same way, at the same point.\n- The read-only tool boundary and the write-tool prerequisites are documented in `apps/api/AGENTS.md`; #133 owns the eventual policy engine that a write-capable filing tool would need.\n",
