            "content": "## Problem\n\n**llame tells the model nothing about when \"now\" is.** Verified: `apps/api/src/prompts/chat-default.md` contains no date or time, and `PROMPT_CONTEXT_PATHS` in `apps/api/src/instance-config/prompt-loader.ts` exposes no temporal path. Nothing anywhere in request assembly carries a clock.\n\nThree consequences, in rising order of cost:\n\n1. **Relative expressions are unresolvable.** \"Tomorrow\", \"next week\", \"by end of month\", \"how long ago was that\" have no anchor. The model either guesses from its training cutoff — which is wrong by construction and confidently so — or asks.\n2. **Absolute dates in context are uninterpretable.** #307's recency digest lists each chat's last-activity date. Without a \"now\", `2026-07-20` cannot be placed: the model cannot tell yesterday from last year except by guessing. The feature's entire purpose is recency awareness, and the recency signal currently has no reference point.\n3. **Temporal planning degrades.** Sequencing, deadline reasoning, and \"what should I do first\" all rest on knowing where the present sits. This is the weakest-evidenced of the three and the one worth measuring rather than asserting.\n\nRelative dates are not the fix and would be worse. A frozen or cached \"3 days ago\" rots into a confident lie — the exact failure documented in [`docs/research/long-term-memory/2026-07-27-user-context-injection.md`](../blob/master/docs/research/long-term-memory/2026-07-27-user-context-injection.md) §2, where the studied product's inferred memories carry relative phrases pinned to long-past observation dates.\n\n## Outcome\n\nThe model knows the current date, anchored well enough to resolve relative expressions and to place dated context, without a per-turn timestamp at position 0 destroying prefix caching for the entire conversation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **A snapshotted temporal anchor in the system prompt**, resolved when a chat is created and refreshed **at compaction only** — deliberately the same lifecycle as #307's recency digest, and for the same reasons. A model switch must **not** refresh it: a switch is a provider boundary, not a context boundary, and refreshing there would strand nothing but would change the anchor as a side effect of an unrelated action.\n- **The anchor must be honest about its own staleness.** It is not \"now\", it is \"when this conversation started\" (or \"when it was last compacted\"). Phrasing it as the current time would make a day-old chat assert a wrong date with full confidence, which is worse than no clock at all. Something closer to *\"this conversation began on YYYY-MM-DD\"* is truthful under freezing; the model can reason forward from it and knows not to treat it as the present instant.\n- **Evaluate a fresher value on the message rail.** A timestamp prepended to the triggering user message sits after the cached prefix, so a per-turn value invalidates only the tail — the same slot the model-switch and tool-availability reminders already occupy. That is the only place an actually-current clock is affordable. Decide whether it ships alongside the snapshot or instead of it; a per-turn reminder on every message is a standing token cost for a value most turns do not need.\n- **Timezone is the open problem, and it is not incidental.** Server time is all llame has: timezone was deliberately cut from the personalization work, so no user timezone is stored. A server-time anchor is wrong for any user not colocated with the instance, and silently so. Options — carry an explicit UTC offset and let the model convert, add a user timezone setting, or derive it client-side — should be settled here rather than assumed, because \"the date\" is meaningless without it near midnight and misleading always.\n- **Precision.** Date-only is stable, cheap, and sufficient for the digest's needs. Time-of-day matters for \"this morning\" reasoning but goes stale immediately under freezing. Pick per surface rather than globally.\n- **Renderable path, not a hardcoded line.** Whatever ships should reach the prompt through the existing allowlisted template context so operators control placement and phrasing, consistent with how `model.*` and the per-user paths already work.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] A prompt referencing the temporal path renders an absolute, unambiguous value including its timezone basis.\n- [ ] The value is byte-identical across every run of a chat between compactions, so snapshot reuse and prefix caching are unaffected.\n- [ ] Compaction refreshes it; a model switch does not.\n- [ ] The rendered phrasing does not assert the value as the present instant.\n- [ ] A prompt referencing no temporal path is unaffected, and startup does not fail.\n- [ ] #307's digest dates become interpretable relative to the anchor, asserted by an example rather than assumed.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNo scheduling, no reminders, no cron, no time-based automation. No user-timezone *feature* beyond what the anchor minimally requires — if a timezone setting is needed it is scoped here only to the extent the anchor consumes it. No per-turn timestamp at position 0 under any circumstances: that forfeits caching for the whole conversation, which is the mistake this issue exists to avoid.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- #307 (recency digest) is the immediate consumer: its per-entry dates are close to decorative without an anchor, and it ships an interim compilation-date line in its own block that this work should supersede rather than duplicate.\n- The snapshot/freeze/compaction-refresh lifecycle is specified in #307's design and should be reused verbatim rather than re-derived.\n- Research §4.3 classifies ambient time as inject-class context — cheap, per-request, no storage — and §2 records how relative dates rot when frozen.\n",
