          "text": "Yes — same mechanism, and your framing is cleaner than mine. Snapshot the set, diff against current, no timestamps anywhere. `title_set_at` and soft-deleted pins both become unnecessary.\n\nThree things fall out of it that are worth pinning down, because two of them change what you snapshot.\n\n## 1. Snapshot the told-set, not the rendered baseline\n\nThis is the one place the framing can go wrong. If \"snapshot\" means the frozen 10+10 that's rendered in the prompt, every chat announced via a delta re-fires on the next send — it's in the appends but not in the baseline, so the diff keeps seeing it as new. Infinite repeat.\n\nSo two separate fields with different lifecycles:\n\n| | lifecycle |\n|---|---|\n| **rendered baseline** — the 10+10 with their entries | frozen, immutable until re-bake |\n| **told-set** — every chat this conversation has been told about, plus its last-told pin state | grows with each append, resets at re-bake |\n\nThat's the structural point from finding A, and it survives your simplification. The design currently conflates them into one \"baseline JSONB,\" which reads as a freeze guarantee that isn't true of half the contents.\n\n## 2. The diff has to be asymmetric — and that deletes two rules\n\n- **entered** (currently in the eligible view, absent from told-set) → append\n- **pin state differs** from told state → append\n- **left** the eligible view → **nothing**\n\nThat third line is just the displacement decision, but making it a property of the diff rather than a separate rule means **archival and deletion need no special-casing at all**. A deleted or archived chat simply leaves the eligible set, departures are ignored, nothing is emitted. Two spec rules collapse into one.\n\n## 3. \"Title landed\" isn't the real trigger — \"became visible\" is\n\nYour diff catches something my three-event framing missed. A chat sitting at rank 15, untold, gets a new message and jumps to rank 1. It's now in the eligible view and absent from the told-set, so it fires — correctly. But it didn't gain a title; it **resurfaced**.\n\nThat's good signal (\"the user just went back to that thing\"), arguably better than the new-chat case. But it means there aren't three events. There's one:\n\n> a chat entered the told-set, or its pin state changed\n\nTitle acquisition is just the most common *reason* a chat enters, not the event itself. That simplifies the spec, the renderer, and the tests — and it removes an entire class of bug where a resurfaced chat is silently never mentioned because nobody wrote a rule for it.\n\n## One note\n\nThe told-set stores chat **ids** internally. That's not in tension with dropping ids from the rendered output — bookkeeping identity and rendered content are different things, and it's worth saying so in the design before someone reads the two decisions as contradictory.\n\n---\n\nNet effect on the spec: three events become one, two rules (archival, deletion) become consequences, and no schema changes to `chats` or `pins`. Finding A shrinks to \"separate the two stored fields.\"\n\nWant me to fold this in along with C/B/D/E, or keep grilling?",
